  "grammar": {
    "title": "ce, cet, cette, ces: this, these",
    "intro": "To point at something — « this jumper », « these shoes » — French uses a demonstrative that agrees with the noun. It means this or that: the context tells you which.",
    "table": [
      [
        "",
        "Demonstrative",
        "Example"
      ],
      [
        "masculine",
        "ce",
        "ce pull"
      ],
      [
        "masculine + vowel or h",
        "cet",
        "cet anorak, cet hôtel"
      ],
      [
        "feminine",
        "cette",
        "cette robe"
      ],
      [
        "plural",
        "ces",
        "ces chaussures"
      ]
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "fr": "Ce pull coûte quarante euros.",
        "en": "This jumper costs forty euros."
      },
      {
        "fr": "Cette robe est trop grande.",
        "en": "This dress is too big."
      },
      {
        "fr": "Je prends ces chaussures noires.",
        "en": "I'll take these black shoes."
      }
    ],
    "trap": "cet sounds like cette: « cet hôtel » = « cette hôtel » to the ear, but hôtel is masculine. Colours go after the noun and agree: « une robe verte », « des chaussures noires ». To insist on this one / that one, add -ci or -là: « ce pull-ci », « ce pull-là »."
  },
  "sounds": {
    "title": "ce or ces?",
    "explain": "« ce » has the soft « euh » sound, « ces » has a smiling « é ». You also hear the plural with a liaison before a vowel: « ces_amis ».",
    "pairs": [
      [
        "ce pull",
        "ces pulls"
      ],
      [
        "ce",
        "ces"
      ],
      [
        "cette robe",
        "ces robes"
      ],
      [
        "cet ami",
        "ces amis"
      ]
    ],
    "tip": "Smile for ces, relax your lips for ce."
  },
